Architecture Decision Memo: 
\f1\b0 VAE Recommended for Synthetic Data Generation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl368\sa146\partightenfactor0
\cf2 We recommend a Variational Auto Encoder (VAE) architecture to synthetically augment a training dataset for a diabetic retinopathy classifier primarily due to the project constraint of a limited dataset consisting of 800 real images across five severity levels. VAEs may also help model intermediary disease progression between severity levels via interpolation. \
The primary failure mode of a VAE architecture will be unusably blurry or nonsensical image outputs. While a simple visual inspection of an output sample grid should reveal this failure mode prior to production, plausible, seemingly useable images may fail silently by omitting fine-grained pathological features required by the safety-critical downstream classifier to effectively detect disease. Accordingly, in addition to an initial output visual inspection, we recommend a waterfall methodology of automated (see below) and human SME checks to ensure VAE synthetic data reliably reproduce clinically informative features. \
We rejected GAN and Diffusion architectures\'97despite their potential to produce higher-quality images useful to the downstream classifier\'97as both are far too \'93data hungry\'94 for the 800-image constraint of this project, requiring in the case of the former architecture at least 10K or more images. A GAN architecture would likely enter varietal mode collapse with an 800-image dataset, as its generator would likely fail to learn the data\'92s distribution with so few training examples. While a Diffusion architecture would not fail in such a manner, overfitting would likely occur wherein the model learned to produce high-quality images that closely matched limited training data but failed to represent the real-world universe of images recording the disease and its progression\'97crucial for the safety-critical downstream classifier.\
We propose the use of quantitative evaluation metrics beyond FID such as Reconstruction Error Masking or Anomaly Scoring; these metrics respectively flag local pixel-level errors beyond a certain threshold or VAE generated outputs excessively far from the known training distributions. Aside from failing to detect varietal mode collapse (admittedly less an issue given we are opting for a VAE vs. GAN architecture), FID alone is insufficient as it is a global statistic that may not necessarily penalize an image for failing to represent less common or local yet clinically relevant features that do not survive a VAE\'92s compression and latent-space regularization towards a normal distribution. We also advise simply testing the downstream classifier\'92s performance prior to production on synthetic data scored in such a manner (possibly alongside a limited human review, as suggested above)\'97if the classifier\'92s performance improves, then the VAE had effectively learned clinically relevant features.\
Finally, a VAE may also help model disease progression between severity levels\'a0 by enabling the exploration of the latent space between known samples. While not an objective of this project, it is a possible secondary benefit.\
}